## 1. Introduction
The sustained attack of infectious bacteria has been one of the world’s most momentous health problems [1]. When bacteria come into contact with a wound, they make use of the nutrients that ambience the wound and burst out, growing at a shocking rate, and infecting and festering the wound [2]. After the wound has been infected, abscess formation, wound dehiscence, delayed wound healing, and even blood poisoning can occur [3]. Currently, antibiotics are applied to deal with bacterial infectious illness. However, the recurrence of infections after therapy indicates that numerous failures of antibiotic treatments are caused by the tolerance of bacterial pathogens to antibiotics [4]. Drug-resistant bacteria, which are caused by the abuse of antibiotics [5], are a major health problem because they make antibiotic treatment obsolete and even lead to the death of patients [6]. The World Health Organization has suggested that more than 700,000 human beings die each year due to resistant bacterial infections [7], and many experts have forecasted that if efforts are not made to reduce resistance or develop fresh antibiotics, the number could increase to 10 million by 2050 [8]. In addition, the therapy cost of wound complications is generally exorbitant both in patients and in medical care systems [9]. The global market for advanced wound healing goods for refractory wounds and scars surpasses $\$5$ billion annually [10]. Hence, finding an effective therapeutic strategy for infectious wounds remains a high priority.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a novel type of crystalline porous material, are composed of metal ions connected by organic binding ligands, and have been of escalating interest due to their important features [11]. MOFs are convenient to prepare using cheap Co, and Ni salts as starting materials [12]. A few studies have shown that nickel and cobalt can further provide synergistic effects in electrocatalytic reactions with various special layered structures [13,14]. The super lamellar structure of NiCo-MOF can not only shorten the path of electron transfer and ion diffusion, but also provide a high surface area and a large number of redox sites [15]. Thus, due to the synergistic effect of cobalt and nickel ions in MOFs to provide favorable catalytic activity, Co and Ni bimetallic organic frameworks will show better electrochemical performance than single metal organic frameworks [16]. Additionally, the structures, dimensions and catalytic performance of MOFs can be readily manipulated by simply tuning the metal molar ratio [17]. The copper ions, as cofactors, can stabilize the conformation of nanozymes, neutralize the anions in the microenvironment, decrease the electrostatic repulsion of the reactions, and transfer electrons to accelerate the reactions [18]. Furthermore, compared with Fe, Co and other transition metals, copper-based catalysts have excellent conductivity, which may improve the charge transfer between active sites and reactants [19]. To date, various MOFs with specific sizes, shapes and compositions (for example, bulk MOF crystals, MOF nanosheets, MOF nanowires, MOF nanoparticles, and hierarchical mesoporous MOFs) have been prepared, and special application studies have been carried out [20]. For example, they have been used in gas separation [21], gas storage [22], sensing [23], catalysis [24,25], and biomedical research [9,26], especially as an antibiotic substitute [27–29]. Therefore, some MOFs that act as nanozymes by simulating the function of natural enzymes are expected to achieve biomimetic catalysis in a series of biomedical applications [30].
Very recently, two-dimensional (2D) MOFs have attracted great interest because of their exceptional physical and chemical properties, high specific surface area, ultrathin layer thickness, high aspect ratio, and plentiful exposure [31]. Significantly, the large surface area of 2D MOFs facilitates the entry of substrate molecules into their surface active sites with a smaller diffusion barrier, while most active sites in 3D bulk MOFs are hidden within the framework [32]. As one of the typical reactive oxygen species (ROS)- based strategies, chemodynamic therapy exploits biocatalytic or redox-chemical reactions to generate ROS for pathogenic cell killing [33–35]. For instance, the Fenton-like reaction is an ironmediated catalytic reaction that can increase the ROS level by converting less-toxic hydrogen peroxide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ into highly toxic hydroxyl ions (OH) to effectively enhance oxidative capability [36]. Recent research reported that peroxidase (POD)-like 2D MOFs have excellent potential in ROS-mediated bacterial removal by catalyzing the conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into highly toxic OH, which was recognized as a benign way to oppose drug-resistant bacterial strains to prevent exposure to superbugs [37]. Therefore, compared to 3D bulk MOF, 2D MOF nanosheets provide better potential in biocatalysis applications [38,39]. Despite their great promise, 2D MOF nanosheets still have many challenges. For example, the harsh conditions of high temperature limit the synthesis of 2D MOF nanosheets, and the productivity is too low to limit their realistic applications [31].
Herein, hierarchical trimetallic NiCoCu-based MOF nanosheets (denoted $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}})$ were synthesized on a large scale by using metal acetate as the precursor and 1,4-dicarboxylic acid (1,4-BDC) as the organic ligand, and the molar ratio was controlled by a mild one-pot solution phase method at room temperature. The synthesized ultrathin trimetallic $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ showed the best catalytic performance, which could successfully enhance the generation of toxic OH for antibacterial treatment with a low concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The results reveal that the presented ultrathin trimetallic $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ shows striking antibacterial activity against both gram-positive Escherichia coli (E. Coli.) and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) bacteria in vitro under a low dosage of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $\cdot10^{-4}\ \mathrm{mm})$ . Furthermore, in vivo experiments confirmed that ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ effectually promoted infected wound healing with negligible adverse effects at the given dosage (Scheme 1). To our knowledge, there are only a few reports of ultrathin trimetallic MOF nanosheets as nanozymes for antibacterial therapy in wound healing. This study gives prominence to the promising potential of ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ , which has POD-like activity for antibacterial therapy in bacteria-infected wound healing.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Nickel acetate tetrahydrate $\mathrm{(Ni(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}.4H_{2}O,}$ $99\%$ , cobalt acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}.4\mathrm{H_{2}O},99\%$ , and copper acetate $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C O O})_{2}.4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O},$ $98\%$ ) were supplied by Aladdin Reagent Co. (Shanghai, China). SYTO 11/PI was purchased from KGI Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (Nanjing, China). Luria Bertani (LB) and phosphatebuffered saline (PBS, $10\mathrm{mM}$ , pH 7.4) were purchased from Dingguo Biotechnology (Beijing, China). Dulbecco’s modified Eagle’s medium (DMEM) and fetal bovine serum (FBS) were purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific (Waltham, MA, USA). Cell Counting Kit-8 (CCK-8) was supplied by Wuhan Boster Biological Technology Co., Ltd. (Wuhan, China). Deionized water was purified using a Millipore water purification system with a minimum resistivity of $18.25~\mathrm{M}\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}$ (Billerica, MA, USA). All chemicals and biological reagents were used directly without purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of ultrathin $(N i_{2}C o_{1})_{1-x}C u_{x}$
The series of MOFs controlling the molar ratio are prepared via an uncomplicated and scalable one-pot room temperature solution-phase method. The synthesis of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.9}\mathsf{C u}_{0.1}$ was taken as a typical example. First, $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(0.6~\mathrm{~\mmol~}$ $149.3{\mathrm{~mg}}{\cdot}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm{COO}})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ (0.3 mmol, $74.7\ \mathrm{mg}$ and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}-$ ${\mathsf{C O O}})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{\langle0.1~\mmol}$ , $25.4~\mathrm{\mg}^{\cdot}$ ) were dissolved into N,Ndimethylformamide(DMF) $\mathrm{~\normalfont~10~}\mathrm{\mL}$ , Solution A) and 1,4-BDC (1 mmol, $166.1\ \mathrm{mg},$ was dissolved with N,N-dimethylformamide $5~\mathrm{mL}$ , Solution B), respectively. Then, solution A was immediately poured into solution B under magnetic stirring, and stirred for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ at room temperature. The products $((\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.9}\mathsf{C u}_{0.1})$ were then centrifuged and washed several times with DMF and ethanol, and finally dried in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The synthesis procedures of other $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ were identical by adjusting the molar ratio of the used $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{CH}}_{3}{\mathrm{COO}})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathrm{H}}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{Cu(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ .
## 2.3. Characterization
The X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) pattern was measured on a PANalytic instrument (Empyrean, Netherlands) to characterize the crystal structure. Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy was performed on a Fourier transform infrared spectrometer (IR,
EQUINOX 55, Germany). The surface composition and valence state were investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Nexsa, Thermo Fisher Scientific, United States). The different morphologies and structures of ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM 1400, Japan) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Regulus 8230, Hitachi, Japan). An accelerating voltage of $3~\mathrm{kV}$ and a magnification of $10\times-20~\times$ were used. The corresponding elemental mapping images were obtained using a field emission SEM equipped with an EDS probe and AZtecEnergy analysis software (Oxford Instruments). The thickness of the nanosheets was measured by atomic force microscopy (AFM, dimension fastscan bio, Bruker). Inductive coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometry (ICP-AES, PerkinElmer optima 8300, PerkinElmer Inc, USA) was applied to detect the released Cu ions, which were obtained from the collected solution from $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ ${\mathrm{(n}}=3{\mathrm{)}}$ .
## 2.4. POD-like catalytic activity experiment
First, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was used as the substrate to determine the catalaselike activity of $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cu_{x}}(\mathbf{x}=0,0.1,0.25,0.5,$ in PBS. Under the action of nanosheets, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ could be decomposed into oxygen $(0_{2})$ . The solutions in the test tube, including $1\ \mathrm{PBS}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as the control, 2 $\Nu\dot{\bf l}_{2}\subset\bf{0}_{1}\ +\ H_{2}\bf{0}_{2}$ , 3 $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.9}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.1}\ +\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ 4 $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.75^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.25}\ +\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and 5 $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu_{0.5}}\ +\ \mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}}$ , were reacted at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 5 min. The final concentrations of nanosheets and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were $10~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and $10\mathrm{\mM}$ , respectively. The photos were obtained. Then, in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the POD-like activity of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ was assessed via the catalytic oxidation of $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB). The characteristic absorption peak at $652\ \mathrm{nm}$ is unique to the oxidation state of TMB $(\varepsilon=39000\mathrm{~m}^{-1}\mathrm{~cm}^{-1},$ . In particular, $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $120\ \upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ) and TMB $\left\langle2\mathrm{\mM}\right\rangle$ were mixed in HAc-NaAc $(0.2~\mathrm{M})$ buffer solution $\left(\mathsf{p H4.0}\right)$ . The change in UV–Vis absorption of oxidized TMB with time was tested at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The Michaelis–Menten constant was ciphered according to the equation $\mathsf{V}_{0}=\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{m}}\times[S]/(\mathsf{K}_{\mathrm{m}}+[S])$ , where $V_{0}$ represents the initial velocity ${\cal V}_{\mathrm{m}}$ indicates the maximal reaction velocity, S represents the concentration of substrate, and $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ expresses the Michaelis constant. The above equation was inverted by both sides, and $\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ were received from the corresponding double reciprocal graph. To check the stability of the $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ samples, we dissolved MOFs using PBS, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , NaCl solution, LB medium, DMF and ethanol as suitable solvents. The powder samples were poured into the above solvents. The formed solutions were kept in airtight containers and stored at room temperature. Then the sample solutions were photographed and recorded on the respective days. All the above experiments were repeated three times.
## 2.5. Detection of OH
OH can revert terephthalic acid (TA) to form 2-hydroxy terephthalic acid (TAOH) with the maximum FL peak at $435\mathrm{nm}$ . By monitoring the fluorescence changes of TAOH caused by the oxidation of TA in aqueous solution, the possibility of nanosheet-catalyzed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation of OH was evaluated. The HAc-NaAc $(0.2\mathrm{~M})$ containing only 1 TA, 2 TA and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , 3 TA and nanosheets, 4 only nanosheets, and 5 TA, nanosheets and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were reacted for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ and centrifuged. Then, the fluorescence spectra of the supernatant were evaluated. The concentrations of TA, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and nanosheets were $0.5\mathrm{\mm}$ , $10~\mathrm{{mm}}$ , and $120~\upmu\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , respectively. All the above experiments were repeated three times.
## 2.6. Antibacterial activity in vitro
Bacterial culture: Monoclonal bacteria on solid LB agar medium were transferred to liquid LB medium and cultured at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12-$ $14\mathrm{h}$ at $180\mathrm{rpm}$ . When reaching the logarithmic growth period, the bacterial solution was diluted to $10^{6}\mathrm{CFU}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ .
MIC experiment: The minimum inhibitory concentration (MICs) of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ was determined through standard serial twofold dilutions following methods recommended by the Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. MIC experiments were performed according to standard reference methods. In short, the synthesized $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ were diluted to the required concentration range, namely from $2560~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ to $10~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Each 96- plate hole was inoculated with $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of bacteria and different nanosheet concentrations. After culturing at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12{-}16\mathrm{~h~}$ , $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of TTC $0.5\%$ aqueous solution) was added and then incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $2{\cdot}3\mathrm{~h~}$ . The MIC of $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}/(N i_{2}C o_{1})_{0.5}C u_{0.5}}$ was inferred to be the lowest concentration of nanozymes that inhibited the visible growth of cells, which was verified by TTC (dead cells are not colored).
Hydrogen peroxide concentration test: Experiments to determine $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations were performed in 96-well plates. Each well was inoculated with $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of E.coli (ATCC 25922) and MRSA (ATCC 43300) bacteria containing $10^{6}$ cells per milliliter in liquid medium. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was applied at different concentrations: $10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ , $10^{-5}\mathrm{M}$ , $5\times10^{-5}\mathrm{M}$ , $10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ , $5\times10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ and $10^{-3}\mathrm{M}$ . Cultures were incubated for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ in individual wells. Then they were measured at OD 600 by a multifunctional microplate reader and visual inspection.
Bacterial proliferation and plate-counting assay: The in vitro antibacterial experiments were assigned to seven groups: 1 bacteria, 2 bacteria $+\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , 3 bacteria ${\bf+\boldsymbol{C}}{\bf u}^{2+}$ , 4 bacteria $+\ \mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ , 5 bacteria $+\mathrm{Ni}_{2}C o_{1}+\mathrm{H}_{2}O_{2}$ , 6 bacteria $+(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ , and 7 bacteria $^+$ $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu_{0.5}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}}$ . The final concentrations of $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\subset\Lt_{0}/(\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}-$ $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ were $120\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and $10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ , respectively. For the bacterial growth inhibition experiment, the above treatment groups were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ . In the platecounting experiments, the bacterial suspension was diluted $10^{3}$ times, and $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of the diluted bacterial suspension was spread on solid medium and cultured in a $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ incubator for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . All the above experiments were repeated three times.
Live and dead cell fluorescence test: Live bacteria were stained with SYTO 11 and showed green fluorescence. The dead bacteria were stained with PI and showed red fluorescence. After washing 2–3 times with PBS, the different experimental groups were treated with SYTO 11 and PI dark staining for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ , and then washed several times with PBS. Finally, an Olympus IX73 fluorescence inverted microscope (Olympus, Japan) was used to observe living cells and dead cells. All the above experiments were repeated three times.
Preparation of bacterial samples for SEM: After different treatments, all treated bacteria were washed with PBS three times. Then, $2.5\%$ glutaraldehyde was used to fix the bacteria, which were stored in a refrigerator at $4^{\circ}C$ overnight. Subsequently, the bacteria were washed several times with PBS and dehydrated with different concentrations of ethanol solution ( $10\%$ $30\%$ , $50\%$ , $70\%$ , $80\%$ and $95\%$ for sample preparation. Finally, the morphological changes were observed by SEM. All the above experiments were repeated three times.
## 2.7. Cytotoxicity assays
The Cell Counting Kit-8 (CCK-8) detection method was as follows. CCK-8 was used to detect cell viability according to the manufacturer’s instructions. Macrophages were seeded in 96-well plates and incubated with 10,000 cells/well for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . A series of concentration gradients of $\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}/(N i_{2}C o_{1})_{0.5}C u_{0.5}}$ were added and cultured for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The OD value and cell viability were measured at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ after CCK-8 solution was added to each well and incubated for $1{-}4\mathrm{~h~}$ The control percentage was used as an indicator to measure cell viability. The following formula was used to calculate cell death: Inhibition $(\%)=((\mathsf{A}-\mathsf{B})/\mathsf{A})\times100$ (A - control group, B - treated group).
## 2.8. Antibacterial analysis of wound in vivo
To evaluate the antibacterial performance of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ in the application of infectious wound healing, we successfully established an infectious wound model. C57BL/6 mice were purchased from Guangdong Medical Laboratory Animal Center. The experiments were approved by the Animal Experiment Center of Guangdong Pharmaceutical University (gdpulacspf2017523). All animals were maintained and used in accordance with the Animal Management Rules of the Ministry of Health of the People’s Republic of China and the Guidelines for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals of China. The infection wounds on the backs of C57 mice (8–10 weeks) were grouped as follows: 1 blank, 2 carboxymethyl cellulose sodium (CMC-Na) gel $^+$ PBS, 3 CMC-Na gel $+\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , 4 CMC-Na gel $+\ (\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ and 5 CMC-Na gel $^+$ $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}+\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ with six-ten mice in each treatment group. MRSA $(5\times10^{7}\thinspace\mathrm{CFU}\thinspace\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , $50\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was injected into the wound to establish infectious wounds. Subsequently, different gels were used to cover on the infected wounds of mice and were replaced every $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Meanwhile, the healing of infected wounds was recorded every two days, and the wound tissue of mice was removed on the 7th day. The wound tissue and main organs of mice were fixed with $10\%$ formaldehyde, and then paraffin sections were made. Finally, hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining and Masson staining were conducted on the wound epithelium. All animal procedures complied with the guidelines of the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee.
## 2.9. In vivo biosafety
$(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ $(2.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ ) was injected into mice by tail vein. Then, on the 7th day, blood samples were taken from the blood vessels of mouse eyes for blood biochemical examination. The main organs of mice treated with $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ or PBS were taken for H&E staining and pathological observation.
