## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of ultrathin $(N i_{2}C O_{1})_{1-x}C u_{x}$
$(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ is synthesized through a compendious and scalable one-pot room temperature solution phase method (Fig. 1a). Fig. S1a shows representative photographs of the as-synthesized $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ with various Cu molar ratios. The color of these products changed from light blue to dark blue with varied Cu molar ratios. However, compared with $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ , there is a thick blue layer of crystals with shiny surfaces for $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.25}\mathsf{C u}_{0.75}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.1}\mathsf{C u}_{0.9}$ because of the presence of excess copper ions (Fig. S1b).
Then, the structures of $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C O_{1}})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cu_{x}}\left(\mathrm{x}=0,0.1,0.25,0.5,0.75\right.$ , 0.9) were analyzed by XRD. XRD patterns of ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathbf{\dot{X}}=0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) are isomorphic to the previously reported Ni-based MOFs with the space group of $C2/m$ (no.985792, Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre) [40,41]. The XRD patterns of ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ ( $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}=\mathbf{\boldsymbol{0}}$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) in Fig. 1b were consistent with the previously reported standard XRD patterns of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ nanosheets [42]. The corresponding peaks of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathbf{\check{X}}=\mathbf{0}$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) appeared at $8^{\circ}-10^{\circ}$ , and each characteristic peak was consistent with the literature report [43]. The XRD spectrum has five peaks at $8.13^{\circ}$ , $15.01^{\circ}$ , and $17.29^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the crystal planes of the [2 0 0], [0 0 1], and [2 0 1] $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ structures, respectively [44]. However, in the XRD patterns, a clear difference between $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ can be observed, which is mainly caused by the intrinsic properties of metal ions. It should be noted that as the molar ratio of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ increases, the (201) peak of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})\mathbf{x}\mathsf{C u}_{1-\mathbf{x}}$ is sharper. The diffraction peaks are sharp and intense, indicating high crystallinity. Hybrid cations usually lead to distortions in the crystal structure, thereby increasing the free holes in the network backbone, and promoting performance changes in MOFs [45]. The organic ligand used in the synthesis of the four materials is PTA, which is similar to the metal acetate [42].
For the FT-IR spectra in Fig. 1c, the stretching vibration of the $^{0-\mathrm{H}}$ bond showed characteristic peaks at $3374.8~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .There are coordinated species in these MOFs. The asymmetric and symmetric vibrations of the carboxyl group and the ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ bending vibration of the benzene ring corresponded to the absorption peaks at $2931.1\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $813.8~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $750.2\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively. According to the existence of the benzene ring skeleton vibration $({\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}})$ , the absorption peaks at $1627\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be deduced. The notable strong peak at $1577.5\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1382\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belong to carboxylate $(\mathsf{C O O^{-}})$ . In addition, a shrunken peak at $3606~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be attributed to the conformity of the connection group between metal ions and PTA, proving the coordination structure of MOFs [46]. However, the peaks of $8^{\circ}-10^{\circ}$ and $15^{\circ}-20^{\circ}$ for $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.25}\mathsf{C u}_{0.75}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.1^{-}}$ Cu0.9 in XRD deviate from the peaks of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ , and there was also no sharp peak at $3606~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Fig. S2). It may be suspected that the synthesis of those nanosheets was not successful, which may be due to the saturation caused by the large molar ratio of Cu ions. Therefore, the ultrathin trimetallic MOF nanosheets of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathbf{x}}$ $\mathbf{\dot{X}}=0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) can be used for subsequent experiments.
The morphology of $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cu_{x}}(\mathbf{x}=0,0.1,0.25,0.5.$ ) was first examined by TEM. Fig. 2a–d and S3 show the TEM images of representative ultrathin $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ , $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.9}\mathsf{C u}_{0.1}$ , $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.75}\mathsf{C u}_{0.25},$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ with uniformly wrinkled nanosheets. The nanosheets provide more exposed active surfaces for more charge storage due to their flexibility and wrinkle properties [47]. The higher-resolution TEM image (Fig. 2e) shows that $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ exhibits well distributed nanosheets. The AFM images indicate that the thickness of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ was between 2.64 and $3.88~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2f), which was similar to the result in a previous report [48,49]. The thickness of the ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ was between 0.52 and $0.63~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 2g). To further characterize the morphology of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathbf{\widetilde{x}}=0,0.1,0.25,0.5$ ), the SEM images explain that ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ contains Ni, Co, C, N, O and Cu elements without other impurities, and the corresponding elements are uniformly distributed throughout the samples (Fig. 2h). The ultrathin $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ contains Ni, Co, C, N, and O elements (Fig. 2i). Furthermore, the four kinds of nanosheets show flake-like morphology (Fig. 2j). The shape of nanosheets did not change by qualifying the molar ratio, denoting that the ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathbf{\partial}_{\cdot}\mathbf{X}=0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) were well maintained.
The chemical bonding state of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathbf{x}}$ $\mathbf{\partial}_{\cdot}\mathbf{X}=0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) was characterized utilizing XPS, as shown in Fig. 3. The survey scan spectrum (Fig. 3a) demonstrates the presence of Ni, Co, O, and C elements in ultrathin $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ , while ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathbf{x}}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{X}}=0.1$ , 0.25, 0.5) has Cu elements in addition to the above elements. The element contents of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ ( $\mathbf{\partial}_{\cdot}\mathbf{X}=0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) are listed in Table S1. All samples displayed similar $\mathtt{C u\ 2p}$ XPS spectra (Fig. 3b), where the main peaks appear in the region at approximately $933\ \mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to the 2p 3/2peaks of Cu. Fig. 3c shows that the bonding state of ${\mathsf{N i}}\ 2{\mathsf{p}}$ is the same in the samples. The peaks at 856.25 and $873.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ can be associated with Ni 2p 3/2 and Ni 2p 1/2, and the other two broad peaks located at approximately 861.4 and $880.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ are determined as their satellites (‘‘Sat.’’), respectively. Similarly, in Fig. 3d, the typical peaks at 780.9 and $797.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ are equivalent to $\mathtt{C o}2\mathtt{p}3/2$ and $\mathtt{C o2p}$ $1/2$ , and the corresponding satellite peaks are at 785.6 and $797.3\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . The above characteristic bands belong to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ , respectively. Thus, the Ni and Co ions in the as-synthesized MOF nanosheets mainly exist in the form of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ and $C o^{2+}$ . We also compare the Ni 2p and $\mathtt{C o2p}$ spectra among ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathbf{x}}$ $(\mathbf{x}=0,0.1,0.25,0.5\$ ) [50]. These results illustrate that Ni, Cu and Co ions are primarily in the valence state of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ in the synthetic ultrathin $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathbf{x}}$ $\mathbf{\partial}_{\mathbf{X}}=\mathbf{\partial}_{0}$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5). The spectrum of C 1 s can be separated into three peaks at 284.7, 285.9, and $288.54\ \mathrm{eV}$ , namely ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-C}}{\mathsf{=}}{\mathsf{C}},$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , and $\scriptstyle0-{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bonds (Fig. S4a). The spectrum of O 1s can be distinguished into two peaks (Fig. S4b), corresponding to the metal oxygen bonds MAOAM (531.67 eV) in the metal oxides and the oxygen in $-0\mathsf{H}$ (533.10 eV). N 1s in $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ ( $\mathbf{\partial}_{\cdot}\mathbf{x}=0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) indicates a broad peak composed of a new metal nitride peak at $400.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ and an $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ peak at 401.1 eV (Fig. S4c), which is slightly transferred to the N species with low energy in metal nitrides. According to previous reports, it may be mainly from the decomposition of DMF into dimethylamine [51]. All the results demonstrate the successful formation of trimetallic $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathbf{\left(x\right)}~=~0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5). Furthermore, we investigated the release rate of copper ions from the $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ nanosheets. The results indicated that $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ released $83.4\%$ of the loaded copper ions within $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ and that the nanosheets could stably and slowly release copper ions for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ without any significant change (Fig. S5).
## 3.2. POD-like activity of $(N i_{2}C O_{1})_{1-x}C u_{x}(x=0,0.1,0.25,0.5)$
In a first suite of experiments, we used TMB and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to judge the catalytic ability of POD-like enzymes as substrates to challenge the catalytic ability of the prepared MOFs [52]. The catalase-like activity of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathbf{x}}$ $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}=0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5) was investigated, and nanosheets can decompose $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into $0_{2}$ and generate bubbles [53]. As denoted in Fig. S6, $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{2}\subset{\bf0}_{1}\ +\ \mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O}_{2}$ and the blank group almost did not produce bubbles (image a, b). Compared to the $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Co_{1}})_{0.9}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.1}\ +\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C O_{1}})_{0.75}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.25}\ +\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (image c, d) groups, more gas bubbles were released in tubes containing $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathrm{Co}_{1}\mathrm{)}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.5}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (image e), indicating that $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ had superior catalase-like activity than the other MOFs.
We subsequently applied the catalytic oxidation of TMB to investigate the POD-like activity of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{x}}~=~0$ , 0.1, 0.25, 0.5). In the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the oxidation peak of TMB will appear at $652\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , which is used as an indicator [54]. Four nanosheets exhibited distinct catalytic activity in the test solution. As shown in Fig. 4a, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ does not have POD-like activity. Compared with $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.9}\mathsf{C u}_{0.1}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.75}\mathsf{C u}_{0.25}$ , $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ exhibited remarkable catalytic performance in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figs. 4a and S7), and the presence of visible blue oxidation products (ox TMB) was observed in $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ Fig. 4b displays the time-dependent catalytic reaction kinetics between different nanosheets. Figs. S8 and S9 show that the absorption values of different nanosheets increased significantly with the accumulation of time within $20\ \mathrm{min}$ . $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C u}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $(\mathbf{x}~=~0.1$ , 0.25, 0.5) has outstanding POD-like catalytic activity upon the addition of copper ions. Under the same conditions, $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.9^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C u}_{0.1}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.75}\mathsf{C u}_{0.25}$ manifested remarkably lower PODlike activity than $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ , reporting vigorous catalytic properties. The promoted enzyme-like activity could be attributed to the synergetic effect of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ ions, which are used as cofactors of nanozymes. The enzyme-like activity was more prominent with an increase in the molar ratio of copper ions [18].
The POD-like activity of nanosheets was further researched by enzyme kinetics theory. Typical enzyme kinetics were obtained by monitoring different reaction times in a specific concentration range of TMB or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . From the results, the catalytic reaction rate increases with increasing concentration, where the reaction rate increases linearly at low substrate concentrations and reaches a plateau at higher substrate concentrations. (Fig. 4c, d and S10). For catalytic reactions, the Michaelis–Menten constant $\left(\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ represents the affinity of the substrates to the enzyme. The $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ value is low when the substrate affinity is high [55]. Judging from the Lineweaver-Burk equation and the double reciprocal plots of the initial reaction rates, the $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ is identified as $0.334\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~a~}$ for the TMB substrate and $1.08\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~a~}$ for the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ substrate (Fig. S11), suggesting that $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ has a good affinity for TMB and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ . Thus, the $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ nanosheet has the strongest catalytic performance compared with other nanosheets (Table S2). The strong enzyme-like activity of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ should be attributed to its own characteristics. On the one hand, compared with large nanoparticles, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5^{-}}$ $\mathtt{C u}_{0.5}$ nanosheets exposed more catalytic active sites, and significantly enhanced the catalytic performance of nanosheets [56]. On the other hand, benefitting from their ultrathin thickness, nanosheets could also lower the mass transfer resistance for catalytic reactions [54]. We then proceeded to explore the stability of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ . The nanosheets were placed in PBS, $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , NaCl solution, LB medium, DMF and ethanol for different numbers of days, and their POD-like enzyme activity was determined. $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ has good stability and retains part of the catalytic ability of MOF in the organic solvent DMF solution and absolute ethanol (Fig. S12).
To explore the catalytic mechanism of $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{1-\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Cu_{x}}\left(\mathrm{x}=0,0.1$ , 0.25, 0.5), we carried out fluorescence experiments. The reaction process diagram of the experiment is illustrated in Fig. 4e. The conversion of nonfluorescent TA into highly fluorescent TAOH was caused by the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and the catalytic performance of nanosheets. The characteristic fluorescence signal was displayed at $435\mathrm{nm}$ , which confirmed the generation of OH [57]. At a given excitation wavelength, only TA, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}+\mathsf{T}\mathsf{A}$ and nanosheets $+\mathsf{T A}$ had no fluorescence (Fig. S13), and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{T}\mathrm{A}+\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1}$ with a negligible fluorescence was observed (Fig. 4f). The results that the fluorescence value of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{TA}+(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Co}_{1})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.5}$ is the highest. It might be possible that the POD-like activity of nanosheets catalyzes the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into OH. $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ are a promising POD-like nanozyme for the conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into OH.
## 3.3. Antibacterial activity in vitro
To further validate the antibacterial performance in vitro and POD-like activity of the synthesized nanosheets, we performed studies of infectious bacteria samples where both Gram-negative E. coli and MRSA were used for assaying in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The high concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ has a bactericidal effect, but it causes serious oxidative stress to tissues, which may cause unnecessary damage to tissues. Prior to this, we conducted in vitro bacterial sensitivity experiments to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ at different concentrations, with the aim of screening out $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ concentrations that are almost nontoxic to bacteria or tissues. As illustrated in Figs. S14 and S15, when the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is $10^{-4}\mathrm{M}$ , the ability to kill bacteria is extremely low. Whether against E. coli or MRSA, approximately $80\%$ of the bacteria survived. The MIC concentration of the nanosheets was determined in vitro. The MIC values of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}.$ - $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ and $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ against E. coli and MRSA bacteria were both $160~\upmu\mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , as shown in Fig. S16.
Referring to the MIC value of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ , we further examined the antibacterial efficiency of $120\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ . In the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the antibacterial activities in vitro against E. coli and MRSA were evaluated. The antibacterial activities of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ were investigated by a growth inhibition test in a liquid medium. When exposed to PBS, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ alone, the survival rates of $E.$ . coli and MRSA were comparatively high (Fig. 5a and b). $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ with or without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ had a very strong killing effect on bacteria. The reason may be the toxicity of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ themselves rather than their ability to produce low amounts of hydroxyl ions [58]. The growth inhibition of bacteria by exposure to $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ alone denoted low antibacterial activity. Upon cotreatment with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ nanosheets, the survival rate of E. coli decreased significantly profiting from the production of toxic OH, which was $13.99\%\pm\:0.69\%$ . Subsequently, the plate counting images indicated the same results (Fig. 5c and d). It can be inferred that the designed $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ could reform a low concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with natural nontoxicity into significantly toxic OH and the dramatic antibacterial activity of OH.
We then proceeded to verify the antibacterial properties of the nanosheets using SYTO11/PI staining for live/dead detection and found that the nanosheets retained their strong bactericidal ability (Fig. 5e and f). Most E. coli and MRSA survived in the PBS group, and there were no red fluorescence signals. After treatment with ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ alone, only a small number of red fluorescent cells were detected due to the low antibacterial ability. $\mathsf{N i}_{2}$ - $\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ nanosheets with or without $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ show a predominant red fluorescence signal, with the number of dead cells increasing significantly, suggesting their enormous toxicity [58]. In the case of cotreatment with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ , the number of dead cells increased remarkably due to the effect of OH (Fig. 5e and f). The red fluorescence signal is mainly shown in the figure, revealing that it has a strong antibacterial ability. The results were the same as those measured by diffusion plate (Fig. 5c,d), which further verified that the antibacterial activity of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ was significantly improved in the presence of nontoxic and harmless $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . The bacterial species were subsequently characterized by SEM to treat the morphological changes caused by $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 5g and h, untreated E. coli is typically rodlike, while MRSA is spherical and smooth. When treated with $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}-$ $\mathrm{Co}_{1}\mathrm{)}_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.5}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , the integrity of the cells was destroyed due to the production of toxic OH, indicating a strong antibacterial effect against E. coli. Almost the same finals were also observed with MRSA (Fig. 5g and h). The results reveal that $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ could effectively kill bacteria at low concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ because it generated toxic OH, causing damage to various bacteria. On the one hand, $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ has a POD-like activity that can generate toxic OH with low concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , resulting in oxidative stress to destroy bacteria [59]. On the other hand, toxic OH wrecks cell membrane permeability, allowing the leakage of nutrients in cells and other substances. In addition, DNA and enzymes can be damaged by ROS, which causes destruction of cell structure and molecular cross-linking, loss of division and proliferation, and even cell death [60].
## 3.4. Evaluation of antibacterial activity in vivo
To evaluate the catalytic ability and antibacterial properties in accelerating the healing of infectious wounds, we first analyzed the in vitro cytotoxicity of nanosheets as a preliminary assay for biosafety. A cell experiment was performed to demonstrate the biocompatibility of $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ and $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ (Fig. S17). As we expected, $\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1}$ has strong toxicity, which limits its further research and application [58]. Then, we studied whether $(\mathsf{N i}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ can stand out in the treatment of skin wound infection and accelerate wound healing in mice. To generate the model, we injected MRSA cells into full-thickness excised wounds in mice. The process of treatment measures is shown in Fig. 6a, and we successfully formed an infectious wound model on the backs of mice and made different gel dressings (Fig. S18). After 3 days of MRSA infection, the wounds of different treatments exhibited varying degrees of redness, ulcer and suppurative properties. In contrast, after treatment with the $\left(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu}_{0.5}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}+\mathrm{CMC}-\mathrm{Na}$ gel dressing, the infected wounds of the mice gradually healed without festering or inflammation. After 5 days of treatment, the wound gradually scabbed and basically healed. The $(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}})_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu_{0.5}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}}+\mathrm{CMC}-$ Na gel dressing significantly accelerated the healing process compared with the control group (Fig. 6b), which proved that OH caused serious oxidative damage to wound bacteria. Importantly, there was no special change in the weight of all mice, indicating that the designed $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ had no cytotoxicity (Fig. 6c). Moreover, on the seventh day, H&E staining was performed on the liver, heart, spleen, lung and kidney of mice. The results showed that no damage or inflammation was observed. (Fig. S19). In addition, the healing rate of infected wounds was also observed and recorded, as shown in Fig. 6d.
To further understand the changes in wound healing tissue, Masson staining and H&E staining were used for histological analysis. Due to no antibacterial activity, the blank group and the control group showed many incomplete epidermises (Fig. 6e and f). However, OH can cause serious oxidative damage to wound bacteria. After treatment with the $\left(\mathrm{Ni_{2}C o_{1}}\right)_{0.5}\mathrm{Cu_{0.5}}+\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}}+\mathrm{CMC-Na}$ gel dressing, the wound appeared to have a complete epidemic structure (Fig. 6e and f). Only some inflammatory cells emerged on the wounds of this treatment group. The healing process commonly undergoes four stages: hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and maturation. During healing with satisfactory effects, the numbers of lymphocytes and neutrophils increase in response to inflammation [61]. Thus, the prepared $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can prevent inflammation and further accelerate epithelial reformation, indicating that they have excellent wound healing ability in vivo and provide promising prospects for future clinical applications.
## 3.5. Biocompatibility study
Biosafety experiments can be performed to verify in vivo biocompatibility, and we injected the prepared $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ into mice via the tail vein. There was no special change in the weight of mice, demonstrating that $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ did not produce toxic effects (Fig. S20). At the same time, blood biochemical analysis showed that there were no abnormalities in heart, liver, or kidney function. Serum alanine aminotransferase (ALT), urea albumin (ALB), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), globulin (GLOB), gamma-glutamyl transpeptidase (GGT), total protein (TP), albumin to globulin ratio (AG), creatinine (CR), alkaline phosphatase (ALP)
and total bilirubin (TBIL) were normal in Fig. 7a. To compare the aggressiveness of the toxicity between the two groups, we extracted the major organs of the mice at the end of the study and sent them for histological examination. No obvious organ abnormalities or inflammation were inspected from H&E staining of different tissues, which confirmed that the designed nanosheets had no cytotoxicity (Fig. 7b). These findings indicate that the toxicity of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ in vitro and in vivo is negligible and it has excellent biocompatibility.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, we have successfully fabricated novel ultrathin trimetallic MOFs with modulated atomic ratios via a scalable one-pot room temperature solution phase method for the fast and large-scale synthesis of NiCoCu-based MOF nanosheets. By controlling the molar ratios, the synthesized nanosheets have different morphologies and sizes with different catalytic properties, where the addition of copper ions as cofactors enhances the high POD-like activity. Specifically, $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ excelled in the catalytic production of toxic hydroxide ions from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , showing the best catalytic performance compared to other similar nanosheets. Low concentrations of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ $(10^{-4}~\mathrm{{M})}$ are utilized to produce toxic OH to accelerate bactericidal efficiency in vitro in the presence of MOF nanosheets. The combination of $(\mathsf{N i}_{2}\mathsf{C o}_{1})_{0.5}\mathsf{C u}_{0.5}$ with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can improve wound healing with excellent antibacterial activity and exhibit negligible biotoxicity at a given concentration. Compared with conventional 2D MOFs such as CuTCPP [37,54], $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{PdTCPP})$ [62], and UiO-67 [63] reported so far, this study demonstrated that ultrathin NiCoCu-based MOF nanosheets can be synthesized rapidly on a large scale at room temperature with high catalytic activity for clinical antimicrobial therapy (Table S3). This work will further stimulate widespread interest in the future exploration of unique MOF nanosheets for clinical applications of nanocatalytic antimicrobial therapy.
